      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "Our Sun is a bit of a loner."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.065.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Stars in pairs",
      "body": "Many stars orbit a companion. Our nearest neighbor, Alpha Centauri, is a system of three stars, including Proxima Centauri. Even Sirius has a small, faint partner, Sirius B, which is a white dwarf.",
      "callout": "Alpha Centauri: 3 stars"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.065.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Weighing stars",
      "body": "You can’t put a star on a scale, but two stars orbiting each other obey the laws of gravity. Measure the size and timing of their orbit, and you can calculate their masses. Binary stars are how astronomers first weighed stars. Some pairs even pass in front of each other, dimming like clockwork: eclipsing binaries.",
      "callout": "Orbit size + period → mass"
    },
